$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A marriage that produced four cases</h2>
<p>The parties, Christians married at Coimbatore in 2009, had two sons born in 2010 and 2012. The mother left for her maternal home in Madurai in May 2014, and the litigation that followed ran on four tracks: the husband&rsquo;s divorce petition (decreed on cruelty in 2018 and never appealed), the wife&rsquo;s maintenance case (allowed the same day), the husband&rsquo;s guardianship petition under the Guardians and Wards Act (allowed in his favour), and a Section 498A prosecution against the husband&rsquo;s family that ultimately ended in acquittal. Only the custody track reached the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The question, precisely framed</h2>
<p>The Court put the issue in one sentence: should the custody of children who have lived continuously with their mother since 5 May 2014 be transferred to the father, a natural guardian against whom no disqualification is established? The framing matters, because it concedes the father&rsquo;s legal position entirely — and still answers the custody question against him.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>The guardianship logic</strong><br/>Father is the natural guardian; no unfitness proved; mother&rsquo;s allegations about his mental health unsupported; decree follows title.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>The welfare logic</strong><br/>Ten years of continuity with mother and grandparents; strong natural bond; children mature enough to express preference; disruption itself is a harm. Welfare is the sole and predominant criterion.</div>
</div>
<p>Drawing on the settled line of authority — including the principle that children are neither chattels nor playthings of their parents, and that welfare must be read in its widest sense, covering moral, educational and emotional wellbeing and not merely money or physical comfort — the Court held that the children should not be separated from their mother, while emphasising that the father had not been found unfit and deserved his children&rsquo;s company.</p>

<h2>The visitation architecture</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Direction</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Monthly visitation</td><td>Second Saturday and Sunday, Gandhi Museum, Madurai, 10:00 am – 2:00 pm</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vacation meetings</td><td>Pongal and half-yearly vacations, at the mother&rsquo;s place or the Gandhi Museum, on prior intimation by phone or WhatsApp</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mother&rsquo;s obligation</td><td>To facilitate visitation on the fixed dates and times</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Education costs</td><td>Father to be ready to share future educational expenses if the mother asks</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The Court noted a sobering fact: although visitation at the Gandhi Museum had been preserved since 2019, the father had managed to meet his sons only four or five times in ten to twelve years. The structured, specific schedule — fixed venue, fixed hours, named festivals — is designed to make the right real rather than notional.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Continuity is evidence, not inertia.</strong> The longer a stable arrangement has held, the heavier the burden on the parent seeking to disturb it — even a parent armed with a decree.</p>
  <p><strong>Teenagers&rsquo; preferences count.</strong> At sixteen and fourteen, the children&rsquo;s wishes carried real weight. Interviews with the court, where sought, should be prepared for, not feared.</p>
  <p><strong>Ask for specifics in visitation.</strong> Vague &ldquo;liberty to meet&rdquo; clauses produce four meetings in a decade. Fixed dates, venue and hours produce compliance — and a record when it is breached.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. It is not legal advice; custody and visitation outcomes turn on the welfare assessment in each case.</div>
